From 986d9e7fc9db5a5049353a2cc3884e0c44815086 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 00:15:58 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent: float the thread-navigation controls over the
 conversation

The controls at the end of a turn -- copy this response, scroll to the user
message, scroll to the top -- are rendered by `render_thread_controls` as a row
*inside* a list item. That puts them in the scroll flow, so they leave the
viewport together with the turn they belong to. They are therefore out of
reach in exactly the situation that makes them worth having: a long thread,
scrolled away from both of its ends.

This moves all of them onto the conversation container as an absolutely
positioned overlay -- copy, previous user message, top, bottom, in that
order -- and makes the two that belong to a turn relative to the scroll
position instead of to the thread's ends.

The first version of this patch floated only the two navigation buttons and
left copy inline, on the argument that a control floating over the whole
conversation cannot say which response it means. It can, once "which" is read
off the scroll position, and leaving it inline had a visible cost: the inline
copy button of the last turn sat right behind the overlay, covered by it.

- Copy acts on the turn the overlay sits over: the entry under the viewport's
  bottom edge, found by walking `bounds_for_item` from the scroll top. Over a
  user message it takes the turn above, since that is the response just read
  and the reply below has not scrolled into view. It copies what the inline
  button copied -- `get_agent_message_content`, the agent's text for the whole
  turn -- and nothing of the user's message or the tool calls.

- Copy is disabled, not hidden, while that turn is still in progress:
  generating, waiting for a tool confirmation, or holding a pending
  elicitation. Copying then would hand over a partial response, and a button
  that appears and disappears with the agent's status reads as a layout
  glitch. An earlier turn stays copyable while the last one is generated.

- "Scroll to Previous User Message" used to call
  `scroll_to_user_message_index(None, ..)`, which always resolves to the MOST
  RECENT user message, so a second click went nowhere. It now searches back
  from the scroll top, so each click lands one message earlier -- every
  landing puts that message at the top, and the next search starts above it --
  and a manual scroll in between is honoured rather than fought. A user
  message already partly scrolled past counts as the one to return to. It is
  measured, not counted: no click counter to fall out of sync.

- It is disabled rather than hidden when there is nothing earlier to go to,
  which covers a thread with no user message at all. It is NOT disabled while
  generating: moving the viewport is harmless mid-turn and is when it is most
  wanted.

- "Scroll to Bottom" is new, and is the existing `scroll_to_end`.

- `render_thread_controls` early-returns `Empty` while generating or while a
  confirmation is pending. That was right for a row carrying a copy button and
  a turn's stats, and wrong for controls whose job is to move the viewport, so
  the overlay does not inherit it.

- With the buttons gone, every remaining part of the inline row is optional
  and they can all be absent at once: turn stats off, feedback unavailable, or
  simply not the last turn. It now returns `Empty` instead of leaving an
  invisible band of padding where the buttons used to be. It also loses its
  `thread`, `entry_ix` and `copy_response_index` parameters with their last
  use, and the `rposition` search that fed the last one.

- What stays in that row -- the turn's token count and duration -- moves to
  its left end. Right-aligned, as it was beside the buttons, it sat exactly
  under the overlay once the thread was scrolled to its end, the one place
  it is read. The dot after the last stat goes too: it separated the stats
  from buttons that are no longer in the row.

This rides on `ThreadView` being re-rendered on every scroll: the list's
scroll-wheel handler ends in `cx.notify(current_view)`, and the view that
paints the list is this one, so the enabled state and the copy target are
recomputed from the current position without a scroll handler of their own.

The overlay clears the vertical scrollbar the same container draws (`right_4`)
and keeps the inline row's 0.4/1.0 opacity-on-hover, so it reads as the same
affordance relocated rather than a new one.

Not addressed here: in a thread reopened after a compaction, "Scroll to Top"
reaches the compaction summary, not the first prompt. That is the adapter's
history replay stopping at the compact boundary, not this list -- measured
2026-09-26, and it belongs in the adapter.

Pure Zed: nothing here needs the adapter, so like 0010, 0012 and 0014 it rides
the claude-agent-acp-plus flag BY CHOICE rather than by dependency.
---

